@page "/learn/web-worker-template"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>.NET in a Web Worker · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">.NET in a Web Worker</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your WebAssembly page totals a big CSV export. How do you keep it responsive while it works?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a project template, <code>blazorwebworker</code>. It writes the code to run .NET
        in a Web Worker into your project, as source you own. No framework API changed. You could do this in .NET 10,
        but you had to write that code yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Revenue by product</i> report. It downloads last year's order lines as a CSV file, totals them
        in the browser, and lists the top five products.
    </p>
    <p>
        A clock on the page ticks ten times a second. Each run shows how long it took, and how many ticks the clock
        managed. Fig. 1 runs the work on the page's UI thread, the way a .NET 10 app would. It behaves the same on
        .NET 10 and .NET 11.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The report on the UI thread" Url="demo/web-worker/order-lines/net10"
                Label="No worker: on the UI thread" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            When it says <b>Ready</b>, press <b>Total on the UI thread</b>. The clock freezes until the results appear,
            and it ticked <b>0</b> times. While it runs, every clock on the page stops. Then
            press <b>Total, pausing every 1,000 lines</b>: the clock keeps ticking, but the run takes longer.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>One thread does everything</h2>
    <p>
        Blazor WebAssembly runs your C# on the page's UI thread, the one that also draws the page and handles clicks.
        That's still true in .NET 11. The thread does one job at a time:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The browser's UI thread, while the report runs</span>
        <pre class="snip">UI thread <span class="dim">(one per tab)</span>
├─ TotalOrderLines(csv)   <span class="err">runs to the end, seconds</span>
├─ clock tick             <span class="dim">waits</span>
├─ draw the page          <span class="dim">waits</span>
└─ your click             <span class="dim">waits</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The totalling code never pauses to let anything else run. The work isn't slower here. It's just in the way.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Run the same method in a Web Worker</h2>
    <p>
        A Web Worker is a second thread the browser gives you. Create a worker library from the template, reference
        it from your WebAssembly client, and call your method by name:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">dotnet new blazorwebworker -n NorthwindOps.Worker</span>

<span class="dim">// NorthwindOps.Worker/WorkerMethods.cs</span>
<span class="hl">[JSExport]</span>
public static string TotalOrderLines(byte[] csv) { ... }

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- NorthwindOps.Client.csproj --&gt;</span>
<span class="hl">&lt;ProjectReference Include="../NorthwindOps.Worker/NorthwindOps.Worker.csproj" /&gt;</span>

<span class="dim">// The client component</span>
worker = await <span class="hl">WebWorkerClient.CreateAsync(JS)</span>;
var json = await worker.<span class="hl">InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;</span>(
    "NorthwindOps.Worker.WorkerMethods.TotalOrderLines", [csv]);</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>WebWorkerClient</code> is a class the template adds to the library. It starts a second copy of .NET in
        the worker and sends your data across. The method runs there, so the UI thread stays free.
    </p>
    <p>
        The template also adds two scripts, and <code>WebWorkerClient</code> loads them itself, so you don't add
        any script tags. Here's the same report with the worker.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same report, in a Web Worker" Url="demo/web-worker/order-lines"
                Label=".NET 11 template: in a Web Worker">
        <Caption>
            When it says <b>Ready</b>, press <b>Total in a Web Worker</b>. You get the same top five, the clock never
            misses a tick, and the run takes about as long as Fig. 1's first one. Press <b>Call a misspelled
            method</b> to see a typo (<code>TotalOrderLine</code>) fail at run time with "Method not found".
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Both reports call the same method. Here are the three files:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Client/WebWorker/OrderLineReportNet10.razor" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's component. It calls the same <code>TotalOrderLines</code> method as plain C#, on the UI thread,
            with no worker. The second method is the pausing workaround.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/WebWorker/OrderLineReport.razor" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's component. It starts the worker once, when the component first appears, calls the method by
            name, and disposes of the worker at the end. <code>WebWorkerClient</code> and its methods
            (<code>InvokeAsync</code>, <code>DisposeAsync</code>) come from the template, not from .NET itself.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Worker/WorkerMethods.cs" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            The method both figures run: a static method, marked <code>[JSExport]</code> so JavaScript can call it, that takes bytes and returns JSON.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Pause, offload, or write the worker yourself</h2>
    <p>
        This isn't a .NET 10 bug. On the UI thread the run took 7.9 s. The clock ticked 0 of about 79 times,
        and the page didn't redraw once. You had three ways out:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three .NET 10 workarounds, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">if (++lines % 1000 == 0)
    <span class="hl">await Task.Delay(1);</span></pre>
                <span class="why">Fig. 1's second button: every tick counted (92 of 92), but 9.2 s instead of 7.9 s</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ about 17% slower, and every heavy loop needs its own pauses</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">await Http.PostAsync(
    "api/reports/order-lines", content);</pre>
                <span class="why">send the file to the server and let it do the totals</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ another API to build and secure, and no report when the server can't be reached</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">import { dotnet } from '../_framework/dotnet.js';
const { getAssemblyExports, getConfig } =
    await dotnet.create();
<span class="dim">// ...match replies to requests yourself</span></pre>
                <span class="why">one of five files Microsoft's guide had you write</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ you had to match replies, and handle errors and timeouts, yourself</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The third is the real fix, and it worked in .NET 10. It was just a lot of code to write and maintain.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The worker, written for you</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>dotnet new blazorwebworker</code> writes that code for you: <code>WorkerMethods.cs</code>
        for your methods, <code>WebWorkerClient.cs</code> (69 lines of C#), and two scripts (157 lines).
    </p>
    <p>
        The scripts start the worker, match each reply to its request, turn worker errors into exceptions, and give
        up on a call after 60 seconds. It's your source, not a framework API, so you can read and change all of it.
        That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Not faster, just elsewhere.</b> The worker runs the same code at the same speed. Copying the data across adds a little time.</li>
        <li><b>WebAssembly only.</b> Reference the library from the client project. It does nothing for components that run on the server.</li>
        <li><b>Called by name.</b> A typo or a rename only fails when the call runs, as Fig. 2 shows. Only simple values cross over (strings, numbers, byte arrays), so send JSON for anything else.</li>
        <li><b>A timeout stops the wait, not the work.</b> <code>CreateAsync</code> and <code>InvokeAsync</code> both take a <code>timeoutMs</code> argument, 60 seconds by default. Pass a bigger one for long jobs. When a call times out, the method keeps running in the worker. Your next call has to wait until it ends.</li>
        <li><b>Keep one worker.</b> Each worker is a whole second copy of .NET. Start one, reuse it, and dispose of it when the component goes.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
